--TEST--
A settings literal may ask for a weaker `tls` and is refused rather than quietly given the stronger one, and the SQLite arm is a shape the checker accepts and this build cannot open — `rule:core-classes/db-capabilities`, `rule:core-api/shape-arms-are-disjoint`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Db\Tls
// `rule:core-classes/db-capabilities`'s third closed hole. PHP's `pdo_pgsql` defaults to
// `sslmode=prefer` and connects in plaintext whenever the server says so; here
// the weak spelling exists, is writable, and is answered by a refusal that
// names what it asked for. Verifying more than a caller asked for would be a
// promise made quietly, which is the failure mode this is the opposite of.
try {
    var $plain = Core\Db::open({
        driver: Core\Db\Driver::Postgres,
        host: "db.example.test",
        database: "shop",
        user: "app",
        password: "hunter2",
        tls: Core\Db\Tls::Disabled,
    });
    echo "opened without TLS", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

// The refusal above landed before the `db.open` grant was consulted, and this
// deployment grants nothing at all -- so the ordering is the assertion: what a
// settings literal *says* is judged before what a deployment allows, exactly as
// `Core\Db::connect` judges its options before it consults the grant.

// The other arm of `Db\Settings`, which takes a `path` and declares no `host`
// at all. It type-checks, which is what makes it an arm, and it opens -- so
// what refuses it here is the same thing that refused the arm above: this
// deployment grants no `db.open` at all. `rule:core-classes/db-capabilities` asks that grant about the
// *path* on this arm, there being no host to ask it about.
try {
    var $file = Core\Db::open({
        driver: Core\Db\Driver::Sqlite,
        path: "/srv/shop/shop.db",
    });
    echo "opened a file", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

echo "still running", "\n";
--EXPECT--
Core\Db::open: `tls` asks for `Tls::Disabled`, and this runtime opens every TCP connection at `Tls::VerifyFull` — `rule:core-classes/db-capabilities` has no spelling for verifying less. A private certificate authority is a `tls_ca_file` on a `[db.<name>]` block, which changes whose certificates are believed and not whether they are checked
Core\Db::open needs the capability `db.open` for /srv/shop/shop.db, which is not granted
help: grant it in nvs.toml under `[capabilities.db]`
still running
